    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Neurexins (NRXN) are a family of three presynaptic cell-adhesion molecules that regulate synaptic function and organization via binding with myriad postsynaptic partners. The discovery of neurexin mutations in human patients with neurodevelopmental disorders, like Autism Spectrum Disorder and schizophrenia, introduced a greater clinical focus to neurexin research. The link between NRXN mutations and these human disorders remains nebulous, however, due to incomplete understanding of neurexins’ full synaptic role.\r\n\r\nI injected viral CRE into the retinas of newborn mice from a novel triple-neurexin “floxed” line to produce an in vivo, conditional neurexin knockout (cKO) and avoid embryonic lethality. I found that triple-NRXN cKO retina exhibited a significantly decreased excitatory synapse density than their control counterparts. The number of retinal ganglion cell (RGC) axonal projections from triple-NRXN cKO retina to the dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus (dLGN) was also significantly decreased compared to control densities. Surprisingly, this reduction in RGC projections did not correspond to a decrease in excitatory synapse density in the dLGN contralateral to the triple-NRXN cKO retina compared to control. Together, these results indicate a potential role for the neurexin family in facilitating proper synapse formation within the visual system, thereby augmenting our current understanding of these crucial molecules.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
